1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:05,429
First-order linear equations are probably the most useful method in this chapter.

2
00:00:05,429 --> 00:00:10,584
They show up everywhere: circuits, mixing tanks, cooling, growth and decay.

3
00:00:11,404 --> 00:00:15,729
And the trick to solve them is one of the nicest ideas in the course.

4
00:00:15,729 --> 00:00:18,802
We multiply by one special function, and the whole

5
00:00:18,802 --> 00:00:21,761
left side collapses into a single derivative.

6
00:00:25,661 --> 00:00:28,141
How do you spot a linear equation?

7
00:00:28,141 --> 00:00:31,287
y and y prime appear only to the first power.

8
00:00:31,287 --> 00:00:36,550
No y squared, no square root of y, no y times y prime, no sine of y.

9
00:00:36,970 --> 00:00:40,295
Your lecture writes the general form as, a one of

10
00:00:40,295 --> 00:00:44,562
x, dy/dx, plus a zero of x, y, equals g of x.

11
00:00:44,981 --> 00:00:49,478
The coefficients can be any functions of x. That is completely fine.

12
00:00:49,478 --> 00:00:51,013
Only y has to behave.

13
00:00:51,433 --> 00:00:57,533
Then the first step, every single time: divide by a one of x, to get standard form.

14
00:00:57,533 --> 00:01:00,583
dy/dx, plus P of x y, equals f of x.

15
00:01:04,484 --> 00:01:08,391
So why does multiplying by something help? Remember the product rule.

16
00:01:08,811 --> 00:01:14,786
The derivative of mu times y, is mu times y prime, plus mu prime times y.

17
00:01:15,206 --> 00:01:20,935
Now look at our equation, multiplied by mu. mu y prime, plus mu P y.

18
00:01:21,355 --> 00:01:26,265
These match the product rule perfectly, if mu prime equals mu times P.

19
00:01:26,685 --> 00:01:29,808
And that little equation for mu is separable.

20
00:01:29,808 --> 00:01:33,652
Its solution is, mu equals e to the integral of P dx.

21
00:01:33,652 --> 00:01:35,934
That is the integrating factor.

22
00:01:36,354 --> 00:01:41,012
So after multiplying, the whole left side becomes one derivative.

23
00:01:41,012 --> 00:01:43,893
The derivative of mu y, equals mu f.

24
00:01:47,693 --> 00:01:51,097
Here are the three steps, exactly as your lecture lists them.

25
00:01:51,517 --> 00:01:56,094
Step one. Put the equation in standard form, and find P of x, and

26
00:01:56,094 --> 00:01:59,584
the integrating factor, e to the integral of P dx.

27
00:02:00,005 --> 00:02:04,620
Step two. Multiply the standard form by the integrating factor.

28
00:02:04,620 --> 00:02:09,235
The left side is now the derivative of the integrating factor times y.

29
00:02:09,654 --> 00:02:12,904
Step three. Integrate both sides, and solve for y.

30
00:02:13,324 --> 00:02:17,665
Notice, you do not need a constant when you compute the integrating factor.

31
00:02:17,665 --> 00:02:20,037
The one constant shows up in step three.

32
00:02:23,737 --> 00:02:26,452
Let us do Example 4 from Lecture 3.

33
00:02:26,452 --> 00:02:29,554
dy/dx, minus three y, equals six.

34
00:02:29,974 --> 00:02:35,160
It is already in standard form, so P of x is negative three. Careful with that sign.

35
00:02:35,580 --> 00:02:38,187
The integrating factor is e to the integral of

36
00:02:38,187 --> 00:02:41,700
negative three, which is e to the negative three x.

37
00:02:42,119 --> 00:02:47,287
Multiply through. e to the negative three x y prime, minus three e to the

38
00:02:47,287 --> 00:02:51,470
negative three x y, equals six e to the negative three x.

39
00:02:51,890 --> 00:02:56,364
The left side collapses. The derivative of e to the negative three x

40
00:02:56,364 --> 00:02:59,765
times y, equals six e to the negative three x.

41
00:03:00,185 --> 00:03:01,831
Integrate both sides.

42
00:03:01,831 --> 00:03:07,592
e to the negative three x y, equals negative two e to the negative three x, plus c.

43
00:03:08,013 --> 00:03:10,636
Divide by e to the negative three x.

44
00:03:10,636 --> 00:03:15,210
y equals negative two, plus c e to the three x, for every x.

45
00:03:15,630 --> 00:03:20,300
Notice the constant c got divided too, so it multiplies e to the three x.

46
00:03:20,300 --> 00:03:23,803
Dropping that is the most common mistake on this method.

47
00:03:27,503 --> 00:03:30,316
Now Example 5, which tests step one.

48
00:03:30,316 --> 00:03:34,984
x squared minus nine, times y prime, plus x y, equals zero.

49
00:03:35,404 --> 00:03:40,689
y prime is multiplied by x squared minus nine, so divide by it first.

50
00:03:40,689 --> 00:03:43,662
P of x is x over x squared minus nine.

51
00:03:44,082 --> 00:03:49,397
P is not defined at x equals three, or negative three. So we work on one interval.

52
00:03:49,397 --> 00:03:51,364
Say, x greater than three.

53
00:03:51,784 --> 00:03:56,767
The integrating factor is e to the integral of x over x squared minus nine.

54
00:03:56,767 --> 00:04:00,777
That is e to the one half natural log of x squared minus nine,

55
00:04:00,777 --> 00:04:04,484
which is just the square root of x squared minus nine.

56
00:04:04,904 --> 00:04:10,063
Then the derivative of root x squared minus nine, times y, equals zero.

57
00:04:10,063 --> 00:04:13,243
Integrate, and y equals c over the square root of

58
00:04:13,243 --> 00:04:16,482
x squared minus nine, on three to infinity.

59
00:04:20,381 --> 00:04:22,299
Three traps to watch for on the midterm.

60
00:04:22,719 --> 00:04:26,450
One. Forgetting to divide by the coefficient of y prime.

61
00:04:26,450 --> 00:04:31,460
If you build the integrating factor from the wrong P, everything after it is wrong.

62
00:04:31,880 --> 00:04:34,059
Two. Dropping the minus sign.

63
00:04:34,059 --> 00:04:37,758
For y prime minus three y, P is negative three, not three.

64
00:04:38,178 --> 00:04:41,326
Three. Forgetting to divide the constant by mu.

65
00:04:41,326 --> 00:04:44,784
The constant goes inside the bracket, before you divide.

66
00:04:48,684 --> 00:04:51,514
So here is the recipe to take into the midterm.

67
00:04:51,934 --> 00:04:54,963
One. Divide by a one of x, to get standard form.

68
00:04:55,384 --> 00:04:59,161
Two. Build the integrating factor, e to the integral of P.

69
00:04:59,161 --> 00:05:01,298
No constant, and watch the sign.

70
00:05:01,717 --> 00:05:06,208
Three. Multiply through, so the left side is the derivative of mu y.

71
00:05:06,629 --> 00:05:11,255
Four. Integrate, add C, divide everything by mu, and state the interval.

72
00:05:11,675 --> 00:05:16,527
Next up, exact equations, where the answer is a level curve of a hidden function.
